# Lockfiles are generated. Git's line-based merge can produce a file that looks
# resolved and is not: two branches each adding packages will interleave cleanly
# and leave a tree that never existed for any resolver. `npm ci` then either
# fails with "Missing: <pkg> from lock file" or, worse, installs a set nobody
# chose.
#
# -merge marks them as unmergeable, so a collision stops and asks. The fix is
# always to regenerate rather than to edit:
#
#   git checkout --theirs <lockfile> && npm install --package-lock-only
#   git checkout --theirs poetry.lock && poetry lock
#
# See docs/decisions/0005-commit-lockfiles.md.
package-lock.json  -merge linguist-generated
pnpm-lock.yaml     -merge linguist-generated
bun.lock           -merge linguist-generated
yarn.lock          -merge linguist-generated
poetry.lock        -merge linguist-generated
Gemfile.lock       -merge linguist-generated
